# DTF tier manifest: npm-unpublish-and-push-sync  (artifact-keeper#4025, #4643, #4658)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single + the real npm CLI client (client.npm). The
# bugs live in the npm/goproxy handlers and the peer sync enqueue, ahead of any
# storage backend, so the storage dimension is irrelevant.
#
# THE BUGS
#   #4025  `npm unpublish` was a silent no-op. None of the requests the client
#          sends was routed:
#              PUT    /npm/{repo}/{pkg}/-rev/{rev}           packument rewrite
#              DELETE /npm/{repo}/{pkg}/-/{file}/-rev/{rev}  tarball delete
#              DELETE /npm/{repo}/{pkg}/-rev/{rev}           whole package
#          (all 404; npm's encoded scoped form `@scope%2fname/-rev/{rev}` hit
#          the GET-only scoped version route and 405'd). libnpmpublish reads a
#          404 as "already gone" and exits 0, so the publisher believed the
#          version was gone while it stayed served and installable. The npm
#          exit code is therefore NOT the signal: the oracle asserts the served
#          packument, dist-tags, tarball GET and the artifacts row.
#   #4643  npm publish and the goproxy .mod/.zip upload never queued a push sync
#          task, so push/mirror peers never received hosted npm packages or Go
#          modules (Maven and the generic upload did).
#   #4658  a re-upload to an existing path keeps the artifact id, and the
#          enqueue only bumped the priority of the already-completed push row:
#          a versioned Generic/ML repo re-upload, or an upload after a delete,
#          never reached the peer.
#
# THE PEER
# POST /api/v1/peers does not probe the endpoint, so the oracle registers a peer
# at an unresolvable `.invalid` host and subscribes four repos to it in push
# mode. It then sets the peer `online` (the delete fan-out only targets online
# peers) with `backoff_until` a day out (the sync worker never claims tasks for
# a peer in backoff), so every sync_tasks row stays where the enqueue left it.
# "The peer received the first push" is simulated by marking that row
# `completed` in the DB, exactly as the backend's own unit tests do.
#
# Oracle (discriminating, RELEASE_GATE=1):
#   (CONTROL) true on BOTH images: real npm publish of scoped and unscoped
#             packages and a real npm install round-trip; the version kept by an
#             unpublish still installs; an anonymous -rev DELETE removes
#             nothing; a generic upload to a push-subscribed repo queues a push
#             row; a Go upload carrying X-Artifact-Keeper-Replication queues
#             nothing; an unversioned live overwrite is 409 and leaves the
#             completed push row completed; a delete queues a delete row.
#   (FIX)     RED on 1.11.1:
#             #4025 real `npm unpublish pkg@ver --force` (scoped + unscoped):
#                   version gone from the packument, latest recomputed, tarball
#                   404, no live row, ARTIFACT_DELETED audited; real
#                   `npm unpublish pkg --force`: packument 404, install fails;
#                   raw routes: tarball -rev DELETE 200 then 404, no-op
#                   packument PUT 400, packument-minus-version PUT 200,
#                   whole-package DELETE 200 then 404, scoped tarball DELETE
#                   200, encoded scoped DELETE 200.
#             #4643 real npm publish, Go .mod and Go .zip each queue a pending
#                   push row.
#             #4658 versioned re-upload and delete-then-reupload re-arm the
#                   completed push row to pending with retry_count 0.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: the oracle makes ~100 authenticated calls (npm
# publishes/installs, raw probes, uploads); the base default (ON) would throttle
# them and turn an assertion into a 429.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem client.npm"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
